{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","count":16,"questions":[{"id":52230,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Two infinitely long parallel wires carry currents of magnitude $\\mathrm{I}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and are at a distance $4 \\mathrm{~cm}$ apart. The magnitude of the net magnetic field is found to reach a non-zero minimum values between the two wires and $1 \\mathrm{~cm}$ away from the first wire. The ratio of the two currents and their mutual direction is","options":["$\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}=9$, antiparallel","$\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}=9$, parallel","$\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}=3$, antiparallel","$\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}=3$, parallel"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fhb_mTwTQt2R2jLG1-xTEI__lhVF8QwVD3ASwHeumXDs.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fhb_mTwTQt2R2jLG1-xTEI__lhVF8QwVD3ASwHeumXDs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fhb_mTwTQt2R2jLG1-xTEI__lhVF8QwVD3ASwHeumXDs.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FwttYzLOrOVUExLgNRdd83NcO_hhXjgSepCyQzgTCIsM.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FwttYzLOrOVUExLgNRdd83NcO_hhXjgSepCyQzgTCIsM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FwttYzLOrOVUExLgNRdd83NcO_hhXjgSepCyQzgTCIsM.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\mathrm{B}_{\\mathrm{p}}=\\frac{\\mu_{0} \\mathrm{I}_{1}}{2 \\pi \\mathrm{x}}+\\frac{\\mu_{0} \\mathrm{I}_{2}}{2 \\pi(4-\\mathrm{x})}\\frac{\\mathrm{dB}_{\\mathrm{p}}}{\\mathrm{dx}}=0$ for minima of $\\mathrm{B}_{\\mathrm{p}}\\Rightarrow \\frac{\\mu_{0} \\mathrm{I}_{1}}{2 \\pi}\\left[\\frac{-1}{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}\\right]+\\frac{\\mu_{0} \\mathrm{I}_{2}}{2 \\pi} \\frac{1}{(4-\\mathrm{x})^{2}}=0\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}{\\mathrm{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}{(4-\\mathrm{x})^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}{\\mathrm{I}^{2}}=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{x}}{4-\\mathrm{x}}\\right)^{2}\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}=\\left(\\frac{1}{4-1}\\right)^{2}\\frac{\\mathrm{I}_{2}}{\\mathrm{I}_{1}}=\\frac{9}{1}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213983ec9bf3a827ee1"},{"id":52231,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"In an experiment, setup A consists of two parallel wires which carry currents in opposite directions as shown in the figure. A second setup B is identical to setup A, except that there is a metal plate between the wires\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FG34Z_-VKk2LrYVEGfP2aPgjlqKPHSezPWhE1XfzARwU.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FG34Z_-VKk2LrYVEGfP2aPgjlqKPHSezPWhE1XfzARwU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FG34Z_-VKk2LrYVEGfP2aPgjlqKPHSezPWhE1XfzARwU.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nLet $\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{A}}$ and $\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}$ be the magnitude of the force between the two wires in setup A and setup $\\mathrm{B}$, respectively.","options":["$F_{A}\\gt F_{B} \\neq 0$","$\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{A}} \\lt  \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}$","$\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{A}}=\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}} \\neq 0$","$\\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{A}}\\gt \\mathrm{F}_{\\mathrm{B}}=0$"],"answer":2,"solution":"In setup B, A metal is placed, due to which metal may get magnetized and it may also exert force on current carrying wire but force between two wire remain same however net force on wire may get charge due to magnetic field produced by magnetized metal.\nAns. (C)","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2136cafa5c3669d1937"},{"id":52232,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"An electron enters a chamber in which a uniform magnetic field is present as shown\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FFhESXlWVYZalMgxbC8ZvGOjuyqPsDOiNgDQci37C7VE.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FFhESXlWVYZalMgxbC8ZvGOjuyqPsDOiNgDQci37C7VE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FFhESXlWVYZalMgxbC8ZvGOjuyqPsDOiNgDQci37C7VE.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nAn electric field of appropriate magnitude is also applied so that the electron travels undeviated without any change in its speed thorugh the chamber. We are ignoring gravity. Then, the direction of the electric field is","options":["opposite to the direction of the magnetic field","opposite to the direction of the electron's motion","normal to the plane of the paper and coming out of the plane of the paper","normal to the plane of the paper and into the plane of the paper"],"answer":3,"solution":"$\\mathrm{q} \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{E}}+\\mathrm{q}(\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{V}} \\times \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{B}})=0$\nHence, into the paper.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21302412da489806767"},{"id":52233,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A wire bent in the shape of a regular n polygonal loop carries a steady current I. Let 1 be the perpendicular distance of a given segment and $R$ be the distance of given segment and $R$ be the distance of a vertex both from the centre of the loop. The magnitude of the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is given by.","options":["$\\frac{n \\mu_{0} I}{2 \\pi l} \\sin (\\pi / n)$","$\\frac{n \\mu_{0} I}{2 \\pi R} \\sin (\\pi / n)$","$\\frac{n \\mu_{0} I}{2 \\pi l} \\cos (\\pi / n)$","$\\frac{n \\mu_{0} I}{2 \\pi R} \\cos (\\pi / n)$"],"answer":0,"solution":"\nB=\\frac{\\mu_{0} i}{4 \\pi l} \\times 2 \\sin \\theta\n\nWhere $2 \\theta=\\frac{2 \\pi}{n}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2135e86841491af61aa"},{"id":52234,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>The current in flowing along the path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> of a cube (shown in the left figure) produces a magnetic field at the centre of cube of magnitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>. Dashed line depicts the non-conducting part of the cube.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fbec26c15-4643-4c33-bb4d-e1aa2d54c546-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fbec26c15-4643-4c33-bb4d-e1aa2d54c546-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fbec26c15-4643-4c33-bb4d-e1aa2d54c546-image.png?alt=media\"/><br/>\nConsider a cubical shape shown to the right which is identical in size and shape to the left. If the same current now flows in along the path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math>, then the magnitude of magnetic field at the centre will be</p>\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fbec26c15-4643-4c33-bb4d-e1aa2d54c546-image.png?alt=media\"/>","options":["zero","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><mi>B</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>B</mi></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>Given magnetic field due to a square loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>A</mi></math> at centre of cube is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math>.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fc6cb9572-0b6d-47fd-b1f5-e02ee4c17c0f-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6cb9572-0b6d-47fd-b1f5-e02ee4c17c0f-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6cb9572-0b6d-47fd-b1f5-e02ee4c17c0f-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>Now, loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>E</mi><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></math> can be viewed as super position of three square loops as shown below.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F10ac75dc-6cfd-48c8-9a18-33485a71a0c3-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F10ac75dc-6cfd-48c8-9a18-33485a71a0c3-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F10ac75dc-6cfd-48c8-9a18-33485a71a0c3-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p>So, net field at centre<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo></math>  resultant of fields of these three loop</p>\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F8ed6a17a-d643-410b-9b3f-8a1ec672e445-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F8ed6a17a-d643-410b-9b3f-8a1ec672e445-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F8ed6a17a-d643-410b-9b3f-8a1ec672e445-image.png?alt=media\"/></p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"italic\">net</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mi>B</mi><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></math></p>\n<p>Magnitude of resultant field is</p>\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>net</mi></msub></mfenced><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mo>·</mo><mi>B</mi></math></p>\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fc6cb9572-0b6d-47fd-b1f5-e02ee4c17c0f-image.png?alt=media\"/>\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F10ac75dc-6cfd-48c8-9a18-33485a71a0c3-image.png?alt=media\"/>\n<img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F8ed6a17a-d643-410b-9b3f-8a1ec672e445-image.png?alt=media\"/>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6ac76b5379fb699abbe"},{"id":52235,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>A collimated beam of charged and uncharged particles is directed towards a hole marked <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> on a screen as shown below. If the electric and magnetic fields as indicated below are turned on</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F4ff3863a-50ab-4927-967f-a3607b2ab646-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F4ff3863a-50ab-4927-967f-a3607b2ab646-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F4ff3863a-50ab-4927-967f-a3607b2ab646-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 324px; height: 184px;\" /></p>","options":["only particles with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></math> will go through the hole <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>","only charged particles with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></math> and neutral particles will go through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>","only neutral particles will go through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>","only positively charged particles with speed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>E</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>B</mi></math> and neutral particles will go through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>For charged particles</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fda94ae6e-00bc-4e5c-9760-4f2bb8e31df2-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fda94ae6e-00bc-4e5c-9760-4f2bb8e31df2-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fda94ae6e-00bc-4e5c-9760-4f2bb8e31df2-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 143px;\" /></p>\n\n<p>net force is in downward direction, so they won't be able to go through the hole <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>\n\n<p>And uncharged particle don't deviate so they will be able to go through hole <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da753fcdef249e94fa875"},{"id":52236,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A rectangular region of dimensions $\\mathrm{w} \\times l(\\mathrm{w}\\lt  \\lt  l)$ has a constant magnetic field into the plane of the paper as shown. On one side the region is bounded by a screen. On the other side positive ions of mass $\\mathrm{m}$ and charge $\\mathrm{q}$ are accelerated from rest and towards the screen by a parallel plate capacitor at constant potential difference $\\mathrm{V} \\lt  0$, and come out through a small hole in the upper plate. Which one of the following statements is correct regarding the charge on the ions that hit the screen?<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FZFZOaL1lYtCmckDhkOTv2h-uhlPhuhAsaRmmO3bqIXI.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FZFZOaL1lYtCmckDhkOTv2h-uhlPhuhAsaRmmO3bqIXI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FZFZOaL1lYtCmckDhkOTv2h-uhlPhuhAsaRmmO3bqIXI.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","options":["Ions with $\\mathrm{q}\\gt \\frac{2|\\mathrm{v}| \\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{w}^{2}}$ will hit the screen.","Ions with $\\mathrm{q} \\lt  \\frac{2|\\mathrm{v}| \\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{B}^{2} \\mathrm{w}^{2}}$ will hit the screen.","All ions will hit the screen.","Only ions with $q=\\frac{2|v| m}{B^{2} w^{2}}$ will hit the screen."],"answer":1,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F023DULasbrd1r_Mh2kMMu8wSotKQK2y4UPzyz27X4Uo.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F023DULasbrd1r_Mh2kMMu8wSotKQK2y4UPzyz27X4Uo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F023DULasbrd1r_Mh2kMMu8wSotKQK2y4UPzyz27X4Uo.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nIons will hit if $\\mathrm{r}\\gt \\mathrm{W}$.\n$\\begin{array}{l}\n\\mathrm{w}=\\mathrm{q} \\Delta \\mathrm{V} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mv}^{2}=\\mathrm{q} \\mathrm{V} \\\\\n\\mathrm{v}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\mathrm{qV}}{\\mathrm{m}}} \\\\\n\\frac{\\mathrm{mv}^{2}}{\\mathrm{r}}=\\mathrm{qvB}\n\\end{array}\n$\\begin{aligned} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{r} &=\\frac{\\mathrm{mv}}{\\mathrm{qB}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{qB}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\mathrm{q} \\mathrm{V}}{\\mathrm{m}}} \\\\ \\mathrm{r}=\\frac{1}{\\mathrm{~B}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\mathrm{mV}}{\\mathrm{q}}} \\\\ & \\frac{1}{\\mathrm{~B}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\mathrm{mV}}{\\mathrm{q}}}>\\mathrm{w} \\\\ & \\frac{2 \\mathrm{mV}}{\\mathrm{q}}>\\mathrm{w}^{2} \\mathrm{~B}^{2} \\\\ \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{q} & < \\frac{2 \\mathrm{mV}}{\\mathrm{w}^{2} \\mathrm{~B}^{2}} \\end{aligned}","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2131f9a670cf7ba1762"},{"id":52237,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A conducting rod of mass $\\mathrm{m}$ and length $l$ is free to move without friction on two parallel long conducting rails, as shown below. There is a resistance $R$ across the rails. In the entire space around, there is a uniform magnetic field B normal to the plane of the rod and rails. The rod is given an impulsive velocity $\\mathrm{v}_{0}-$\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FHpIIl_ji0KYhw_ftewmL4M1R3SMLyUMTVaGeDddDQF4.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FHpIIl_ji0KYhw_ftewmL4M1R3SMLyUMTVaGeDddDQF4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FHpIIl_ji0KYhw_ftewmL4M1R3SMLyUMTVaGeDddDQF4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nFinally, the initial energy $\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mv}_{0}^{2}$","options":["Will be converted fully into heat energy in the resistor","Will enable rod to continue to move with velocity $\\mathrm{v}_{0}$ since the rails are frictionless.","Will be converted fully into magnetic energy due to induced current","Will be converted into the work done against the magnetic field"],"answer":0,"solution":"Due to energy conservation","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21368ec733af659e087"},{"id":52238,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A steady current I flows through a wire of radius $\\mathrm{r}$, length $\\mathrm{L}$ and resistivity $\\rho$. The current produced heat in the wire. The rate of heat loss in a wire is proportional to its surface area. The steady temperature of the wire is independent of-","options":["$\\mathrm{L}$","r","I","$\\rho$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Concept of fuse wire","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2012 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213019c1fabd266de97"},{"id":52239,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A rectangular loop of wire shown below is coplanar with a long wire carrying current $\\mathrm{I}$.\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FDVAC0j54g96v-tQeM4GboM1YEQePRa2uPgcHywUf0Y4.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FDVAC0j54g96v-tQeM4GboM1YEQePRa2uPgcHywUf0Y4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FDVAC0j54g96v-tQeM4GboM1YEQePRa2uPgcHywUf0Y4.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nThe loop is pulled to the right as indicated. What are the directions of the induced current in the loop and the magnetic forces on the left and the right sides of the loop?","options":["Induced current - Counterclockwise, Force on left side - To the left, Force on right side - To the right","Induced current - Clockwise, Force on left side - To the left, Force on right side - To the right","Induced current - Counterclockwise, Force on left side - To the right, Force on right side - To the left","Induced current - Clockwise, Force on left side - To the right, Force on right side - To the left"],"answer":1,"solution":"Flux is inward and it is decreasing as loop is going away from wire\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FQlBholteZ5LftDFpmlfaWIBC5mCrEw9dlOa2QxYEGO0.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FQlBholteZ5LftDFpmlfaWIBC5mCrEw9dlOa2QxYEGO0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FQlBholteZ5LftDFpmlfaWIBC5mCrEw9dlOa2QxYEGO0.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\n$\\therefore$ direction of induced current is clockwise<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FC6IJbHNuCVNTd-WOYOqaicH5yq5PY3_MJc4UJIxzQZ8.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FC6IJbHNuCVNTd-WOYOqaicH5yq5PY3_MJc4UJIxzQZ8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FC6IJbHNuCVNTd-WOYOqaicH5yq5PY3_MJc4UJIxzQZ8.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nForce on left side is in left and force on right side is in right.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2132ab4fefb6315a2f0"},{"id":52240,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A charged particle of charge $\\mathrm{q}$ and mass $\\mathrm{m}$, gets deflected through an angle $\\theta$ upon passing through a square region of side 'a' which contains a uniform magnetic field B normal to its plane. Assuming that the particle entered the square at right angles to one side, what is the speed of the particle?","options":["$\\frac{\\mathrm{qB}}{\\mathrm{m}} \\mathrm{acot} \\theta$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{qB}}{\\mathrm{m}} \\mathrm{a} \\tan \\theta$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{qB}}{\\mathrm{m}} \\mathrm{a} \\cot ^{2} \\theta$","$\\frac{\\mathrm{qB}}{\\mathrm{m}} \\mathrm{a} \\tan ^{2} \\theta$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FrhLmmqvWtu7BXSrHEdLO7-NSh1nK2AfmYtMG703hwEg.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FrhLmmqvWtu7BXSrHEdLO7-NSh1nK2AfmYtMG703hwEg.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FrhLmmqvWtu7BXSrHEdLO7-NSh1nK2AfmYtMG703hwEg.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\"><br>\nNow $\\sin \\theta=\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{R}}, \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\mathrm{mv}}{\\mathrm{qB}}$\n\\therefore \\mathrm{v}=\\frac{\\mathrm{q} \\mathrm{Bacot} \\theta}{\\mathrm{m}}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2010 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213f40917ef37fab2cf"},{"id":52241,"subject":"Physics","chapter":"Magnetic Effects of Current","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>The magnetic field is uniform for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>></mo><mn>0</mn></math> and points into the plane. The magnetic field is uniform and points out of the plane for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo><</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></math> A proton denoted by filled circle leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math> in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>y</mi></math>-direction with some speed as shown below.<br/>\n<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ff